(138,1,'2026-04-07 12:15:42','2026-04-07 12:15:42','Welcome to the IEEE Engineering in Medicine and Biology Society (EMBS) Chapter at Khalifa University. The chapter is a vibrant student-led community dedicated to advancing biomedical engineering through innovation, collaboration, and professional development. Our chapter serves as a platform for undergraduate and graduate students and researchers to engage with cutting-edge topics in healthcare technology, connect with experts in the field, and participate in workshops, seminars, and outreach activities. We aim to foster interdisciplinary growth and contribute to the global EMBS mission of applying engineering solutions to improve human health.','Home','','inherit','closed','closed','','12-revision-v1','','','2026-04-07 12:15:42','2026-04-07 12:15:42','',12,'http://127.0.0.1:8001/?p=138',0,'revision','',0),
(139,1,'2026-04-07 12:17:14','2026-04-07 12:17:14','Welcome to the IEEE Engineering in Medicine and Biology Society (EMBS) Chapter at Khalifa University. The chapter is a vibrant student-led community dedicated to advancing biomedical engineering through innovation, collaboration, and professional development. Our chapter serves as a platform for undergraduate and graduate students and researchers to engage with cutting-edge topics in healthcare technology, connect with experts in the field, and participate in workshops, seminars, and outreach activities. We aim to foster interdisciplinary growth and contribute to the global EMBS mission of applying engineering solutions to improve human health.','Home','','inherit','closed','closed','','12-revision-v1','','','2026-04-07 12:17:14','2026-04-07 12:17:14','',12,'http://127.0.0.1:8001/?p=139',0,'revision','',0),
(140,1,'2026-04-07 12:17:30','2026-04-07 12:17:30','Welcome to the IEEE Engineering in Medicine and Biology Society (EMBS) Chapter at Khalifa University. The chapter is a vibrant student-led community dedicated to advancing biomedical engineering through innovation, collaboration, and professional development. Our chapter serves as a platform for undergraduate and graduate students and researchers to engage with cutting-edge topics in healthcare technology, connect with experts in the field, and participate in workshops, seminars, and outreach activities. We aim to foster interdisciplinary growth and contribute to the global EMBS mission of applying engineering solutions to improve human health.','Home','','inherit','closed','closed','','12-revision-v1','','','2026-04-07 12:17:30','2026-04-07 12:17:30','',12,'http://127.0.0.1:8001/?p=140',0,'revision','',0),
(141,1,'2026-04-07 12:18:55','2026-04-07 12:18:55','This session featured two distinguished speakers:\r\n<ul>\r\n 	<li><strong>Dr. Maria Farsari</strong>\r\n<em>Director of Research, Institute for Electronic Structure and Laser, Foundation for Research and Technology-Hellas (IESL/FORTH), Greece</em>\r\n<strong>Talk Title:</strong> <em>“Multiphoton Lithography for Metamaterials Fabrication”</em></li>\r\n 	<li><strong>Prof. Daniele Tosi</strong>\r\n<em>Full Professor, Nazarbayev University, School of Engineering and Digital Sciences, and Head of the Biosensors and Bioinstruments Laboratory, National Laboratory Astana</em>\r\n<strong>Talk Title:</strong> <em>“Optical Fiber Biosensors Based on Semi-Randomized Interferometry: Towards Multifunctional Detection Probes”</em></li>\r\n</ul>\r\nThis engaging seminar showcased cutting-edge advancements in photonics, metamaterials fabrication, and biosensing technologies.\r\n\r\n&nbsp;','Lasers in Life Sciences: From 3D Bio Printing to Sensing','','publish','closed','closed','','lasers-in-life-sciences-from-3d-bio-printing-to-sensing','','','2026-04-08 07:52:36','2026-04-08 07:52:36','',0,'http://127.0.0.1:8001/?post_type=embs_event&#038;p=141',0,'embs_event','',0),
(142,1,'2026-04-07 12:19:28','2026-04-07 12:19:28','The College of Medicine and Health Sciences (CMHS) at Khalifa University held its inaugural CMHS Undergraduate Research Conference on May 2nd, 2025. This milestone event was organized in collaboration with the IEEE Engineering in Medicine and Biology Society (EMBS) Student Chapter and the ASBMB Student Chapter, with the goal of showcasing cutting-edge undergraduate research in the fields of Biomedical Engineering (BMEB) and Biological Sciences (BS).\r\n\r\nThe conference featured 20 oral presentations and multiple poster exhibitions, highlighting Senior Design Projects, Independent Studies, Student Research Projects, and Mubadala-sponsored work. The organizing committee was chaired by Dr. Gihan Elbait and Dr. Maria De Fatima, supported by faculty supervisors and student volunteers from both chapters.\r\n\r\nThe event commenced with opening remarks by Dr. Kinda Khalaf, followed by an engaging Kahoot research quiz that served as an interactive icebreaker. Presentations then proceeded across both morning and afternoon sessions, covering a diverse range of biomedical and biological topics. A structured judging session was held for both oral and poster presentations, with certificates and awards distributed to recognize outstanding research efforts.\r\n\r\nLogistics, catering, social media promotion, and on-site coordination were managed by designated subcommittees. The event also provided opportunities for interdisciplinary networking and academic collaboration among students, faculty, and guests.\r\n\r\nThis successful first edition set a strong foundation for future undergraduate research events at CMHS, fostering a culture of innovation, scholarly communication, and student leadership.\r\n\r\n&nbsp;','CMHS Undergraduate Research Conference','','publish','closed','closed','','cmhs-undergraduate-research-conference','','','2026-04-08 07:53:41','2026-04-08 07:53:41','',0,'http://127.0.0.1:8001/?post_type=embs_event&#038;p=142',0,'embs_event','',0),
(143,1,'2026-04-07 12:19:34','2026-04-07 12:19:34','This seminar on bioelectronic devices for neural interfacing by Professor Christopher Proctor. His insights into flexible electronics, conducting polymers, and microfluidic integration showcased how these innovations are actually transforming diagnostics and therapy. The seminar focused on the emerging field of bioelectronic devices for neural interfacing, exploring how advanced technologies are bridging the gap between electronics and biological systems. These technologies are paving the way for more effective neural interfaces, with potential applications in medical diagnostics, therapy, and personalized healthcare. \r\n\r\nThe seminar was organized by the EMBS students\' chapter at Khalifa University in collaboration with the Collage of Medicine and Health Sciences (CMHS) and the Biomedical Engineering Department (BME).','Extending the Reach of Bioelectronic Implants','','publish','closed','closed','','extending-the-reach-of-bioelectronic-implants','','','2026-04-08 07:50:47','2026-04-08 07:50:47','',0,'http://127.0.0.1:8001/?post_type=embs_event&#038;p=143',0,'embs_event','',0),
(144,1,'2026-04-07 12:19:49','2026-04-07 12:19:49','On February 25th, the IEEE EMBS Chapter at Khalifa University hosted the Healthcare Innovation Hackathon 2025, bringing together 38 students in nine teams for a full-day challenge focused on transforming biomedical ideas into startup concepts. Participants engaged in ideation, planning, and pitching, with winners receiving cash prizes and certificates.\r\nUnder the theme \"Innovating Healthcare for All,\" students tackled real-world issues through challenges like home-based diagnostics, AI-driven tumor detection, and accessible healthcare tools. The event highlighted the power of combining engineering, AI, and entrepreneurship to drive impactful healthcare solutions.\r\n1st Place Team: Kareem Salem, Miriam Istanboli, and Najwa Kamal. Their idea was\r\nabout developing a smart bandage that uses nano structures similar to butterfly wings to detect infections in real-time. It changes color based on pH, temperature, moisture, and bacterial activity providing power free, at home wound monitoring for faster, safer healing.\r\n2nd Place Team: Mohammed Ruhan Goltay, Mohammad Barazi, Jaafer Alzaiwary, Abedalqader Arafat, and Moath Sabri. Their idea was about KineticX which is a smart wearable device designed to support patients with movement disorders by tracking and analyzing mobility patterns in real time. It aims to enhance rehabilitation through personalized feedback and remote monitoring. The device integrates sensor data to empower both patients and clinicians with actionable insights\r\n3rd Place Team: Shahad Alkaabi, Buttia Almansoori, Maitha Aldhaheri. Their idea was about Memora which is a fun and supportive mobile app designed for individuals with Alzheimer’s, combining engaging games and a reward system to encourage daily cognitive stimulation. The app also tracks and monitors user progress, promoting consistency and a sense of achievement.','Healthcare Innovation Hackathon 2025','','publish','closed','closed','','healthcare-innovation-hackathon-2025','','','2026-04-08 07:49:17','2026-04-08 07:49:17','',0,'http://127.0.0.1:8001/?post_type=embs_event&#038;p=144',0,'embs_event','',0),
(145,1,'2026-04-07 12:19:55','2026-04-07 12:19:55','On February 18, 2025, the Khalifa Innovation Center hosted an inspiring panel discussion on Women in Emerging Technology, featuring leading voices in AI, Robotics, HealthTech, and Biotechnology. The event brought together distinguished speakers including Prof. Ray Johnson, Lucie Berger, Dr. Kinda Khalaf, Dr. Giulia De Masi, Dr. Maryam Matar, and Dr. Maria De Fatima Domingues, who shared their personal journeys, challenges, and insights on navigating and excelling in the tech world. With engaging talks, an interactive Q&A session, and vibrant networking opportunities, the session offered a powerful platform to explore the future of women in STEM and inspire the next generation of innovators.','Women in Emerging Technology','','publish','closed','closed','','women-in-emerging-technology','','','2026-04-08 07:43:56','2026-04-08 07:43:56','',0,'http://127.0.0.1:8001/?post_type=embs_event&#038;p=145',0,'embs_event','',0),
(146,1,'2026-04-07 12:20:08','2026-04-07 12:20:08','Khalifa University was honored to host Professor Jonathan Knowles from University College London for a research seminar on “Future Biomaterials: Biomedical, Medicine, and Other Applications” on Wednesday, February 19, 2025, at the Main Campus Auditorium. As UCL’s most highly cited materials scientist and a global leader in biomaterials research, Professor Knowles shared insights from his extensive career, which spans over 400 publications and numerous international collaborations.\r\nDuring the seminar, Professor Knowles discussed the evolution of degradable biomaterials, from hydroxyapatite for bone grafting to cutting-edge 3D-printed resins for maxillofacial applications. He also highlighted recent advancements in nerve regeneration using innovative conduits developed with Dankook University. The event offered a valuable opportunity for attendees to explore the intersection of materials science, medicine, and clinical innovation.','The Future of Biomaterials','','publish','closed','closed','','the-future-of-biomaterials','','','2026-04-08 07:47:40','2026-04-08 07:47:40','',0,'http://127.0.0.1:8001/?post_type=embs_event&#038;p=146',0,'embs_event','',0),
(148,1,'2026-04-07 12:20:37','2026-04-07 12:20:37','','Women in Emerging Technology','','inherit','closed','closed','','145-autosave-v1','','','2026-04-07 12:20:37','2026-04-07 12:20:37','',145,'http://127.0.0.1:8001/?p=148',0,'revision','',0),
(149,1,'2026-04-07 12:25:40','2026-04-07 12:25:40','The <strong>IEEE EMBS KU Chapter</strong> played a pivotal role in organizing and hosting the <strong>11th International Conference on Software Development and Technologies for Enhancing Accessibility and Fighting Info-Exclusion (DSAI 2024)</strong>. This event, scheduled for <strong>November 13-15, 2024</strong>, focused on software and web accessibility, human-computer interaction, e-health, and inclusive technologies.\r\n\r\n<strong>Key Contributions:</strong>\r\n<ul>\r\n 	<li><strong>Conference Organization:</strong>The chapter actively collaborated in <strong>planning, logistics, and execution</strong>, ensuring a seamless experience for attendees, speakers, and researchers.</li>\r\n 	<li><strong>Technical Engagement:</strong>Members participated in <strong>peer-review processes, special track coordination, and technical support</strong>, fostering a high-quality academic environment.</li>\r\n 	<li>The event provided a <strong>platform for students and faculty</strong>to engage with cutting-edge research, encouraging interdisciplinary discussions in <strong>AI, accessibility, and e-health</strong>.</li>\r\n 	<li><strong>Global Networking:</strong>By co-hosting this international conference, the chapter <strong>enhanced its global visibility</strong>, attracting renowned researchers and industry professionals.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\nThis event provided the <strong>IEEE EMBS Student Chapter at Khalifa University</strong> with the opportunity to join the <strong>organizing committees</strong> for the <strong>7th International Conference on Activity and Behavior Computing (ABC 2025)</strong> and the <strong>21st IEEE Biomedical Circuits and Systems Conference (BioCas 2025)</strong>, further strengthening the chapter’s role in the global biomedical engineering community.\r\n','DSAI 2024 Conference','','publish','closed','closed','','dsai-2024-conference','','','2026-04-08 07:40:58','2026-04-08 07:40:58','',0,'http://127.0.0.1:8001/?post_type=embs_event&#038;p=149',0,'embs_event','',0),
(150,1,'2026-04-07 12:25:54','2026-04-07 12:25:54','The <strong>\"Mastering Ubuntu and Python: A Hands-On Coding Workshop\"</strong> was a three-day technical event held in <strong>October</strong>, designed to equip students with essential skills in <strong>Ubuntu Linux and Python programming</strong>. This interactive session provided hands-on experience, particularly focusing on <strong>AI applications in the medical field</strong>, aligning with the IEEE EMBS KU Chapter’s mission to enhance technical proficiency in biomedical engineering.\r\n\r\n<strong>Key Objectives:</strong>\r\n<ul>\r\n 	<li>Introduce students to <strong>Ubuntu and Linux-based computing</strong>, essential for biomedical data analysis and AI research.</li>\r\n 	<li>Provide hands-on training in <strong>Python programming</strong>, covering fundamentals, automation, and practical applications.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\nEmpower students to use <strong>Python for medical applications</strong>, bridging coding with real-world healthcare challenges.','Mastering Ubuntu and Python: A Hands-On Coding Workshop','','publish','closed','closed','','mastering-ubuntu-and-python-a-hands-on-coding-workshop','','','2026-04-08 07:06:58','2026-04-08 07:06:58','',0,'http://127.0.0.1:8001/?post_type=embs_event&#038;p=150',0,'embs_event','',0),
(151,1,'2026-04-07 12:26:05','2026-04-07 12:26:05','The IEEE EMBS chapter as well as the Biomedical Engineering Department proudly participated in the Khalifa University Open Day 2025, showcasing student-led projects, interactive demos, and departmental achievements to prospective students and visitors. Highlights included live ECG signal recording, the Stress Stoop Task, and displays of innovative prototypes such as EEG headsets and artificial limbs. Visitors also had the chance to engage with BME alumni from medical, industrial, and academic fields, adding depth to the event. Informative presentations and creative giveaways further enriched the experience, making it a successful and engaging day for all attendees.','Open Day','','publish','closed','closed','','open-day','','','2026-04-08 07:06:14','2026-04-08 07:06:14','',0,'http://127.0.0.1:8001/?post_type=embs_event&#038;p=151',0,'embs_event','',0),
(152,1,'2026-04-07 12:26:11','2026-04-07 12:26:11','The IEEE EMBS Student Chapter led a <strong>campus-wide Breast Cancer Awareness Month campaign</strong>, educating students and faculty about <strong>prevention, early detection, and available support resources</strong>.\r\n\r\n<strong>Impact &amp; Outcomes</strong>\r\n<ul>\r\n 	<li><strong>Expanded Awareness:</strong> Engaged <strong>500+ students and faculty</strong> through informative sessions and interactive booths.</li>\r\n 	<li><strong>Educational Workshops:</strong> Partnered with healthcare professionals to offer <strong>free screenings</strong> and self-exam guidance.</li>\r\n 	<li>Collaborated with local organizations to distribute <strong>informational pamphlets and pink ribbons</strong>, reinforcing awareness.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<strong>Notable Contributions</strong>\r\n<ul>\r\n 	<li>Promoted <strong>early screening</strong> through engaging <strong>Q&amp;A sessions</strong>.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\nStrengthened ties with <strong>healthcare professionals</strong>, enhancing credibility and outreach.\r\n\r\n','Breast Cancer Awareness Month Campaign','','publish','closed','closed','','breast-cancer-awareness-month-campaign','','','2026-04-08 07:05:42','2026-04-08 07:05:42','',0,'http://127.0.0.1:8001/?post_type=embs_event&#038;p=152',0,'embs_event','',0),
(153,1,'2026-04-07 12:26:20','2026-04-07 12:26:20','The IEEE EMBS Student Chapter actively participated in Clubs/Chapters Day at Khalifa University, an annual event designed to introduce students to the diverse technical and professional clubs/chapters available on campus.\r\n\r\nThis initiative served as a platform to showcase the importance of biomedical engineering, promote collaboration, and encourage student involvement in research and innovation.\r\n\r\n<strong>Key Objectives:</strong>\r\n<ul>\r\n 	<li>Increase awareness of the EMBS Chapter and its role in improving biomedical engineering advancements.</li>\r\n 	<li>Engage students in discussions about emerging technologies in healthcare, AI-driven medical solutions, and biomechanics.</li>\r\n 	<li>Recruit new members by highlighting the benefits of participating in IEEE EMBS activities, including workshops, research opportunities, and industry connections.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<strong>Activities during the event:</strong>\r\n<ul>\r\n 	<li>Stroop Color Word Task that measures mental stress.</li>\r\n 	<li>Detecting and printing the ECG signal (Pulse Rate).</li>\r\n 	<li>Arranging the brain layers in the correct order.</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<strong>Impact and Outcomes:</strong>\r\n<ul>\r\n 	<li><strong>Enhanced Membership Growth:</strong> The event successfully attracted new student members, expanding the chapter’s reach within the university.</li>\r\n 	<li><strong>Increased Engagement:</strong> Numerous students expressed interest in upcoming technical workshops, mentorship programs, and research collaborations facilitated by the chapter.\r\n\r\n</li>\r\n</ul>','Clubs & Chapters Day','','publish','closed','closed','','clubs-chapters-day','','','2026-04-08 07:04:55','2026-04-08 07:04:55','',0,'http://127.0.0.1:8001/?post_type=embs_event&#038;p=153',0,'embs_event','',0),
(154,1,'2026-04-07 12:28:36','2026-04-07 12:28:36','Khalifa University was honored to host a research seminar titled \"Computationally Designed 3D Printed Patient-Specific Implants for Orthopedic Applications,\" presented by Dr. Ali Kiapour, Director of Biomechanics Research at Massachusetts General Hospital and Harvard Medical School. The seminar focused on the use of computational modeling and 3D printing technologies to design orthopedic implants tailored to the unique anatomy and loading patterns of individual patients. These patient-specific implants are engineered to optimize post-implantation response, improving both functionality and recovery outcomes.\r\n\r\nThe discussion delved into how computationally designed implants are developed and validated, as well as their application in clinical settings. The seminar provided attendees with valuable insights into the integration of engineering, biomechanics, and medical innovation.\r\n\r\n&nbsp;\r\n\r\n&nbsp;','Seminar on Computationally Designed 3D Printed Patient-Specific Implants for Orthopedic Applications','','publish','closed','closed','','seminar-on-computationally-designed-3d-printed-patient-specific-implants-for-orthopedic-applications','','','2026-04-08 07:03:57','2026-04-08 07:03:57','',0,'http://127.0.0.1:8001/?post_type=embs_event&#038;p=154',0,'embs_event','',0),
(155,1,'2026-04-07 12:33:43','2026-04-07 12:33:43','','highlights','','inherit','open','closed','','highlights','','','2026-04-07 12:33:43','2026-04-07 12:33:43','',144,'http://127.0.0.1:8001/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/highlights.jpg',0,'attachment','image/jpeg',0),
(156,1,'2026-04-07 12:34:00','2026-04-07 12:34:00','On February 25th, the IEEE EMBS Chapter at Khalifa University hosted the Healthcare Innovation Hackathon 2025, bringing together 38 students in nine teams for a full-day challenge focused on transforming biomedical ideas into startup concepts. Participants engaged in ideation, planning, and pitching, with winners receiving cash prizes and certificates.\nUnder the theme \"Innovating Healthcare for All,\" students tackled real-world issues through challenges like home-based diagnostics, AI-driven tumor detection, and accessible healthcare tools. The event highlighted the power of combining engineering, AI, and entrepreneurship to drive impactful healthcare solutions.\n1st Place Team: Kareem Salem, Miriam Istanboli, and Najwa Kamal. Their idea was \nabout developing a smart bandage that uses nano structures similar to butterfly wings to detect infections in real-time. It changes color based on pH, temperature, moisture, and bacterial activity providing power free, at home wound monitoring for faster, safer healing.\n2nd Place Team: Mohammed Ruhan Goltay, Mohammad Barazi, Jaafer Alzaiwary, Abedalqader Arafat, and Moath Sabri. Their idea was about  KineticX which is a smart wearable device designed to support patients with movement disorders by tracking and analyzing mobility patterns in real time. It aims to enhance rehabilitation through personalized feedback and remote monitoring. The device integrates sensor data to empower both patients and clinicians with actionable insights \n3rd Place Team: Shahad Alkaabi, Buttia Almansoori, Maitha Aldhaheri. Their idea was about Memora which is a fun and supportive mobile app designed for individuals with Alzheimer’s, combining engaging games and a reward system to encourage daily cognitive stimulation. The app also tracks and monitors user progress, promoting consistency and a sense of achievement.\n \n<img src=\"http://127.0.0.1:8001/wp-content/uploads/2026/04/highlights-300x200.jpg\" alt=\"\" width=\"300\" height=\"200\" class=\"alignnone size-medium wp-image-155\" />\n\n\n','Healthcare Innovation Hackathon 2025','','inherit','closed','closed','','144-autosave-v1','','','2026-04-07 12:34:00','2026-04-07 12:34:00','',144,'http://127.0.0.1:8001/?p=156',0,'revision','',0),
